WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.560
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.800
You might have noticed that we have a soft
spot for cynical, irreverent, and even nihilist

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:12.880
comedy.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.720
But, we’ve recently been wondering: is there
anything on the other side of our current

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.700
cynicism?

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.900
Is comedy based in earnestness and genuine
human emotion still possible?

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.060
To answer this question we’re turning to
our friend Bo Burnham, a comedian who might

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:31.010
have cracked the code for how to embrace all
the intellectual goodness of postmodern irony.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:35.630
"My voice is so ****ing natural.”

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.550
While offering something with a real human
core.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.719
"Could we get the house lights up for a second,
could you let the lights on stage, let the

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:44.819
artifice fade away, now we're all the same."

00:00:44.819 --> 00:00:49.579
Like a candybar that has a hard shell of cultural
cynicism on the outside, but a warm hug from

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:51.760
your favorite aunt on the inside.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.030
Even though Burnham is still baby-faced, his
work has actually gone through a few distinct

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.030
stages.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.629
While his earliest work incorporates postmodern
cynicism and ironic detachment, — "I adopted

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:08.050
a child from overseas to rescue him from child-labor
factories, and on his very first birthday,

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:09.930
we went to Build-a-Bear Workshop.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.300
Isn't that ironic?"

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.401
His more recent work not only exposes the
meaningless core of contemporary life, but

00:01:17.401 --> 00:01:22.190
also attempts to offer a corrective with introspection
and a search for happiness.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:27.400
"On a scale from one to zero, are you happy?"

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.390
Burnham might be the rare comedic figure able
to offer salvation from the empty cultural

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.180
wasteland that is our modern world.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.610
So, welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The
Philosophy of Bo Burnham.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.610
And…

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.020
I guess, spoilers?

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.010
PART 1: HOW DID WE GET HERE?

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:46.460
To understand why Burnham represents such
an important shift in comedy, we first need

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:51.241
to see how comedy arrived at its postmodern
era, and more importantly, what in the hell

00:01:51.241 --> 00:01:53.530
is meant by “postmodernism.”

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.910
This phrase has become so amorphous that it
can be used to describe everything from cars

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.460
to Scottish beer.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.500
But a more helpful understanding can be found
in the work of Fredric Jameson, whose work

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:08.350
describes postmodernism as “the cultural
logic of late capitalism.”

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:14.060
Which means that each new form of economic
life creates a related form of cultural life.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.990
For example: when you’re a poor college
student, your economic situation dictates

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:23.030
a cultural life of cheap beers, movies in
a dorm room, and paying for dollar cheesy-roll-ups

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:24.630
with your laundry quarters.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:29.020
When you graduate and get a job, you’re
drinking a locally made IPA, paying twenty

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.940
dollars for a movie ticket, and using your
debit card for a cheesy gordita crunch.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.590
A few years later you sell your app, FLUSHFINDER,
that gives you a map of the cleanest public

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:41.890
toilets near you, and you’ll be drinkin’
aged whiskey, sitting courtside, and sending

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:46.550
your scared assistant Adrian to grab you a
crunchwrap supreme combo.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.230
Each economic stage in your life leads to
a new cultural stage.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:54.610
And postmodernism is the cultural logic of
the current stage of economic development,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.850
which Jameson calls late capitalism.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.870
Now, let’s see how Jameson’s ideas can
help us better understand the shifting trends

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.240
in American comedy over the past 70 years.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:08.450
We can do this with a three step process:
economic culture leads to popular culture,

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.540
which leads to comedy.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.900
Let’s start in the fifties: America is in
a post-war boom, the GI Bill has led to record

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.520
numbers of college grads and homeowners, it’s
easy to get a middle class job, and Cuba is

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.950
just a quaint island of resorts and cocktails.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.830
This era’s popular culture is all about
optimism and enjoyment, "What are you doing

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.130
up here, I thought you were downstairs boxing
chocolates?"

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:30.920
"Oh, they kicked me outta there fast."

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:31.920
"Why?"

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.989
"I kept pinchin' ‘em to see what kind they
were," and their comedy sensibility can be

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:38.130
seen in someone like Jack Benny.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.130
"Congratulations, Boss!”

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:40.130
“Why?”

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.150
“You just shined with a peeled potato!"

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:47.010
By the mid-sixties, the boom time is over,
the cold war has people on edge, and the Vietnam

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.120
war is on the horizon.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.760
There were protests, naked hippies, and someone
gave The Beatles LSD.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:57.870
The counterculture had snuck into the popular
culture, and Lenny Bruce is on trial for obscenities,

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.820
while George Carlin is listing them.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.540
“Sh*t, piss, ****, c*nt, c*ck sucker, mother****er,
and… tits.”

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.700
By the eighties, Reagan has promised endless
economic growth, and we’ve got cable TV

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.860
to keep us company twenty-four-seven.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:14.390
New media creates the opportunity for a new
breed of superstar, and while Michael Jackson

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:17.829
sold out arenas with his music, Eddie Murphy
did the same with comedy.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:21.290
But, let’s be honest, the most important
thing is how fly they look in red leather

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:22.290
jackets.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.780
In the nineties, comedians like Jerry Seinfeld
and Ray Romano had gone from successful standups

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.740
to television stars.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.639
Comedy was more mainstream than ever.

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:36.389
MTV, giant cell phones, and economic growth
abounded, and, all the while, comedy rode

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:39.810
this wave with a new optimism and increasingly
goofy spirit.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.500
"We're not worthy!

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.190
We're not worthy!"

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:48.250
But, just like Taco Bell’s nacho fries,
good things can’t last forever, and by the

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:52.990
dawn of the twenty first century, the dot
com bubble had burst, Limp Bizkit was popular,

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.990
and 9/11 left the country wondering if anything
could be funny anymore.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.880
“Can we be funny?”

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.590
“Why start now?”

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:06.490
As the aughts rolled on, things didn’t get
much better.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:11.240
The 2008 financial crisis was the worst since
the great depression, and the weird Scrubs

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:15.150
reboot left us all wondering if nothing was
sacred anymore.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.660
PART 2: COMEDY AS CULTURAL CYNICISM

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:23.570
The political and economic cynicism of the
aughts lead to a new form of cultural cynicism

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:24.570
in the media.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:25.570
“Keep those on.”

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:26.570
“Leave my nipples alone!

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:30.389
If you don’t ****ing like ‘em, go ****in’
squeeze on your dick.”

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:33.630
While mostly known for its groundbreaking
character ‘Prison Mike’ — "The worst

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:39.290
thing about prison was the… was the dementors,
they, were flying all over the place, and

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:42.910
they were scary, and they come down, and they
suck the soul outta your body, and it hoit!"

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:47.810
The Office showed us the mundane and often
meaningless existence of the American worker,

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:52.670
and the rest of television was slowly taken
over by reality shows that were all completely

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:54.990
fake, but nobody seemed to care.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.040
One of the most recent shifts that helps us
understand the effect of late capitalism on

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.840
comedy is what we’re calling “the Adult
Swim phenomenon.”

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:06.740
What started as a way for Cartoon Network
to get stoners to watch their channel while

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:11.449
eating Double Decker Tacos has since become
the home of some of the weirdest, most absurd,

00:06:11.449 --> 00:06:18.320
and most intentionally cringeworthy comedy
to hit American airwaves.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.420
Tim and Eric Awesome Show Great Job showed
us a surreal and absurdist version of the

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:23.610
upside down.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:24.820
"Pizza Mozzarella!"

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:26.630
"Tastes pretty good!"

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:31.100
Eric Andre ironically occupied the space of
the late night host to show how meaningless

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:35.830
the late night model had become while using
interviews with C-list celebrities to highlight

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.440
the vapid nature of contemporary fame.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:42.130
Mr. Neighbor’s House has recently shown
us what happens when Mr. Rodgers is put through

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:43.750
the postmodern cynicism filter.

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:47.919
“A lie is a lie, no matter what the color.

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:50.370
And when you lie, you hurt your brother.”

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:54.389
And of course, our friends Rick and Morty
give us an animated response to the classic

00:06:54.389 --> 00:06:58.620
philosophical question, “What if Back to
the Future was an R-rated movie involving

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:02.650
the multiverse, nihilism, — ‘Nobody gets
it, nothing you think matters matters,’

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.190
and lots of brown liquor?”

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:08.120
Many of these shows weren’t even funny,
at least in the traditional sense.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:12.280
Whereas the nineties and early aughts used
canned laughter to tell our lizard brains

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:18.680
when to laugh, Eric Andre used canned laughter
to create an uncomfortable moment for all

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:19.680
involved.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.340
"Hey Hannibal, did you hear about this?”

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:27.020
Maybe Andre just wants to push the limits
of what we’ll watch or maybe he’s asking

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:30.280
if there is even such a thing as authentic
comedy anymore.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:33.380
We only know one thing for sure though.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:37.090
“You gotta eat the lettuce…

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:39.630
Just, right… straight up eat the lettuce.”

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:44.080
At the same time that Adult Swim is blossoming,
Occupy Wall Street is taking over the streets

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.289
of Manhattan, and young people around the
world are questioning economic and political

00:07:48.289 --> 00:07:52.230
institutions in a way that would make the
mud-covered hippies proud.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:56.750
But unlike their forefathers Lenny and George,
who used political cynicism to infuse their

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:00.340
comedy with an urgency for truth, — “Got
no steel industry left!

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.750
Can’t educate our young people, can’t
get health care to our old people, but we

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:07.010
can bomb the sh*t out of your country, all
right!

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:12.100
Huh?“ — the Adult Swim generation let
this cynicism go from a rallying cry to cynicism

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.039
for cynicism’s sake.

00:08:14.039 --> 00:08:18.610
Rather than a response to a troubled world,
this cynicism denied the existence of real

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:20.740
meaning or truth in the first place.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:23.640
"Jus' say Smith again, it don't matta.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:24.640
Nonadis mattas."

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:29.280
To return to the fourth meal of cultural theory,
Fredric Jameson, this cynical comedy is a

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.610
perfect exemplification of postmodernism.

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:36.700
The same cultural form repeats, but this time
with irony.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:44.580
Or to paraphrase Marx, “first as Johnny
Carson then as Eric Andre eating his own vomit.”

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:49.370
Cynicism and irony lead to a brand of comedy
in which truth, meaning, and human earnestness

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:53.959
are just useless relics from the era of canned
laughter and pesky New York mothers.

00:08:53.959 --> 00:09:02.960
So, are we now stuck in this postmodern feedback
loop of meaninglessness?

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:06.190
Maybe not, but to find out, we have to return
to our friend, Bo.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:07.330
Nope, the other one.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:08.779
She’s pretty, but no.

00:09:08.779 --> 00:09:13.380
No, not the Obama’s Portuguese water dog,
the comedian, lanky white guy in his twenties.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.160
Yes, ****ing finally, jesus.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:49.230
Part 3: FROM IRONY TO SINCERITY IN BO BURNHAM

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:54.480
Bo Burnham is an interesting case, as much
of his early work was shaped by the influence

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.410
of Dutch absurdist Hans Teeuwen.

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:58.940
“Where have you been?”

00:09:58.940 --> 00:09:59.940
“Jail!”

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:00.940
“For what?”

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:01.940
“Rape!”

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:05.200
“Sorry, I uh, I had no idea.”

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.520
And embodied a detached irony which exemplified
the tenents of postmodern comedy.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:15.180
“If Jesus can walk on water, can he swim
on land?“

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:19.510
Because there is no fixed standard of truth
or meaning, Burnham used comedy to point the

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:21.170
audience to this lack.

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:25.050
"Jesus wasn't the messiah, get back, I'm a
heretic, and I'm on fire, it was Oedipus on

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:28.520
those holy nights, and the Holy mother ****ing
Christ."

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:32.940
But in his recent work, Burnham pushes back
against the emptiness of postmodernism in

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:36.570
an attempt to find genuine meaning in things
like love and happiness.

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:41.740
We could say that rather than returning back
to a modern perspective, Burnham pushes through

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:46.459
postmodernism to arrive at a post-postmodern
perspective, where the death of traditional

00:10:46.459 --> 00:10:50.480
meaning is an opportunity for the creation
of new meanings.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:54.899
We can see his progression by considering
his three specials as three distinct stages

00:10:54.899 --> 00:10:56.170
in his comedic development.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:01.149
His first special, ‘Words, Words, Words’
explores the death of art and comedy, the

00:11:01.149 --> 00:11:02.280
follow up ‘what.’

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:07.150
goes a step further and operates under the
principle that meaning itself is dead, and

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:11.250
his most recent special, ‘Make Happy’
works through the death of meaning in an attempt

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:15.120
to see if real meaning and happiness are still
possible.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.019
From the opening minutes of ‘Words, Words,
Words,’ Burnham aims to show the audience

00:11:19.019 --> 00:11:22.470
the facade of entertainment, “It’s all
an illusion.

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:23.910
I’m wearing makeup.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:25.830
I’m wearing makeup, makeup.”

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:29.740
And for Burnham art is dead because it’s
really just the means for something else,

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:32.240
“So, people think you’re funny… how
do we get those people’s money?”

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.660
True to the logic of late capitalism, the
real purpose of art is simply creating opportunities

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:42.330
for making more money, and presumably, eating
Nacho Cheese Doritos Locos Supremes whenever

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:43.330
you want.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:47.330
And when he speculates on what the forefathers
of counter cultural comedy would think, his

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:53.550
conclusion isn’t pretty, “We’re rolling
in dough, while Carlin rolls in his grave.”

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:58.380
By pointing to the death of art, Burnham shows
how comedy has just become a way for charming

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:02.950
narcissists to make as much money as they
can with no imperative for authenticity or

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:04.020
truth.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:07.780
If ‘Words, Words, Words’ is Burnham’s
eulogy for the death of art, he goes a step

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:12.079
further in ‘what.’, and mourns the death
of truth and meaning as such.

00:12:12.079 --> 00:12:14.649
“Art is a lie, nothing is real.”

00:12:14.649 --> 00:12:19.760
And he is aware that in the context of people’s
actual problems, laughter isn’t the medicine

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:21.080
we’ve been sold, “That’s it!

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:25.240
Laughter is the key to everything; it’s
the way to solve all the sadness in the world..

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:28.870
I mean, not for the people who are actually
sad, but the people like us that have to ****ing

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:30.300
deal with them all the time.”

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:34.700
Unlike the Adult Swim comedians, Burnham doesn’t
just embrace the chaos that comes from the

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:38.340
death of meaning, he is asking what it means
for us when meaning dies.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:42.910
"None of you are going to heaven."

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:45.890
And asking whether or not the creation of
meaning is possible.

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:50.810
“Maybe life on earth could be heaven.”

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:58.100
But he has no conclusive answers in “what.”,
and he lets us sit in that space of not knowing.

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:08.640
If ‘what.’ is the point where he starts
to consider that comedy devoid of the aspirations

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:13.680
of any higher meaning is just an equine ATM,
— “But we’ll stop beating this dead

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:17.730
horse when it stops spitting out money, but
until then...”

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:22.250
—“Make Happy” represents Burnham’s
attempt to use comedy not just for criticism,

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.329
but as a vehicle to search for meaning.

00:13:24.329 --> 00:13:29.390
And he makes clear early on that his comedy
is not a mode of escapism, “You are here

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:34.790
because you want to laugh, and you want to
forget about your problems.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:42.310
But I cannot allow it… you should not laugh.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.320
You should not forget about your problems.”

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:49.230
Instead of escape, Burnham tries to use comedy
as an occasion for the audience to truly encounter

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:52.460
their problems, and maybe themselves, in the
process.

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:56.240
And lest you underestimate Burnham’s desire
to make you think about some real sh*t: “The

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:57.639
world is not funny.

00:13:57.639 --> 00:14:05.209
12% of the world’s population does not have
access to clean drinking water.

00:14:05.209 --> 00:14:07.470
The world is not funny.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:10.500
Guy Fieri owns two functioning restaurants.”

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:15.329
And while he has some facts wrong, Burnham
doesn’t wanna use culture as a way to escape

00:14:15.329 --> 00:14:19.600
reality, which is one of Jameson’s worries
about postmodernism&nbsp;— that it removes the

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.110
imperative for us to be critical about the
world around us.

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:27.250
Like a server who lets you know that he’s
up-selling you on donkey sauce not because

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:31.779
it’s good, but because it’ll make him
money, Burnham lets us peek behind the comedians’

00:14:31.779 --> 00:14:34.820
curtain, “Entertainers, they are lying,
and they are manipulating you.”

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:40.040
It’s not all harsh reality for Burnham though,
as he thinks the move past cynicism has some

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:49.529
upside, “We all deserve love, even on the
days when we aren’t our best.

00:14:49.529 --> 00:14:53.870
‘Cause we all suck, but love can make us
suck less.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:15:04.150
We all deserve love, it’s the very best
part about being alive, and I would know,

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:08.260
I just turned twenty five.”

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:13.470
And by adding the last line, he undercuts
his own point, showing us a position that

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:17.410
straddles the line between naive optimism
and ironic cynicism.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:23.170
The shows ends with Bo Burnham’s take on
a Kanye West concert circa 2016, “Can I

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:25.630
say my sh*t?

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:31.389
New York, can I say my sh*t?

00:15:31.389 --> 00:15:35.029
I got lots of sh*t to say.”

00:15:35.029 --> 00:15:38.680
“Can I talk my sh*t, again?”

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:40.110
And while it might start off with jokes, — “Pringles!

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:41.600
Listen to the people!

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:47.019
I’m sure 90% of the complaint letters you
get are about the width of your cans, just…

00:15:47.019 --> 00:15:48.019
make them wider.”

00:15:48.019 --> 00:15:51.800
— he peppers this confession with some more
vulnerable moments — “I don’t go to

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:58.111
the gym, ‘cause I’m self-conscious about
my body, but I’m self-conscious about my

00:15:58.111 --> 00:15:59.111
body, ‘cause I don’t go to the gym...“

00:15:59.111 --> 00:16:03.529
And he closes out with what seems like a genuine
wish for his audience, “Thank you.

00:16:03.529 --> 00:16:04.529
Goodnight.

00:16:04.529 --> 00:16:07.760
I hope you’re happy.”

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:11.470
With ‘Make Happy,’ Burnham harkens back
to the Socratic tendencies from comedians

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:12.720
in a previous era.

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:17.420
Much like the gadfly of Athens himself, Burnham
is urging his audience to truly acknowledge

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:21.910
their problems and unhappiness, as “the
unexamined life is not worth living.”

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:27.410
And like Aristotle, his final words imply
that happiness might be possible, but we have

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:28.639
to work at it.

00:16:28.639 --> 00:16:32.430
What’s most interesting is that Burnham
doesn’t simply reject postmodern comedy

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:36.830
for the sake of something new, instead, he
pushes postmodern comedy to its limits to

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:38.970
see what’s on the other side.

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:43.550
And over the course of these three specials,
we see him transition from irony, to cynicism,

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:44.550
to earnestness.

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:48.649
Or, from the death of art, to the death of
meaning, to the possibility for meaningful

00:16:48.649 --> 00:16:50.800
art after the death of meaning.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:55.190
PART 4: COMEDY AS CURE FOR DESPAIR, OR, IS
RICK SANCHEZ RIGHT?

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:57.840
Now, you might be wondering, “so what?”

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:01.360
Even if Burnham thinks we should try to be
happy, we live in a world where goodness and

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:04.220
truth seem increasingly illusory.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:08.439
And in a world where Nacho Fries aren’t
even on most Taco Bell menus anymore, is it

00:17:08.439 --> 00:17:11.279
even worth moving past postmodern cynicism?

00:17:11.279 --> 00:17:16.149
One telling factor is that Burnham isn’t
the only one trying to inject a post-postmodern

00:17:16.149 --> 00:17:18.049
sincerity back into comedy.

00:17:18.049 --> 00:17:23.109
A great example is the most critically successful
comedy film of 2017, ‘The Big Sick’, which

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:26.809
coincidentally stars Burnham as a comedian
obsessed with success.

00:17:26.809 --> 00:17:31.429
And while it’s a genuinely funny movie,
it explores family, success, and love with

00:17:31.429 --> 00:17:34.129
a sincerity not common in comedy films.

00:17:34.129 --> 00:17:38.229
And it’s safe to guess that the inclusion
of Ray Romano isn’t a coincidence, as we

00:17:38.229 --> 00:17:42.440
see the cranky sitcom dad of the nineties
portraying a complicated and compassionate

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:43.440
human being.

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:47.880
We’ve also seen this shift from irony to
sincerity in shows like ‘Nathan for You’,

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:53.639
"I mean, I do look at you guys, and a part
of me is envious, that, you know, I don't

00:17:53.639 --> 00:17:56.929
have someone in my life that is…

00:17:56.929 --> 00:17:58.820
I'm this close with."

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:03.059
And infusing absurdity with a story about
genuine adult friendship in ‘Detroiters’,

00:18:03.059 --> 00:18:04.270
"You're my best friend!"

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:05.499
"You're my best friend!"

00:18:05.499 --> 00:18:09.879
And even one of the darkest shows out there,
BoJack Horseman, explores the possibility

00:18:09.879 --> 00:18:10.960
of finding real meaning.

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:15.489
"It takes a long time to realize how truly
miserable you are, and even longer to see

00:18:15.489 --> 00:18:17.740
that it doesn't have to be that way.

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:23.229
Only when you give up everything, can you
begin to find a way to be happy."

00:18:23.229 --> 00:18:27.450
While Fredric Jameson has yet to offer an
analysis of the way in which comedy can cure

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:32.090
the cultural illness of postmodernism, it
might just be the case that Burnham’s work

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:36.380
is telling us something about what will happen
on the other side of late capitalism.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:39.669
Either way the Crunchwrap Supreme is here
to stay.

00:18:39.669 --> 00:18:42.899
So, maybe there are eternal truths that we
can all still believe in.

